@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+# Stream stability: first implementation batch
+
+Base: c8bea2642d0e95402f373af55584a3b98cd89417 (main, 2026-10-09).
+
+This batch implements priorities 1–3 of the stability review. It changes the
+shared C core and the Qt frontend's IDR request integration. It does not change
+bitrate, rendering backend, codec, socket buffer sizes or the default (disabled)
+setting for automatic IDR requests after an FEC failure.
+
+## 1. Packet statistics
+
+- Establish the sequential baseline from the first observed packet.
+- Use 16-bit modular arithmetic across sequence wrap.
+- Track unique sequence numbers with an 8 KiB bitmap per statistics instance.
+- Ignore packets belonging to an already finalized reporting interval.
+- Protect updates, snapshots and resets with the same mutex.
+- Clamp sequential losses to zero if the received count exceeds the span.
+
+Reordered packets received before an interval closes repair gaps in that
+interval. A late packet cannot retroactively modify a report already sent to
+the console. As in the sequence-number helpers, ordering assumes advances of
+less than half the 16-bit sequence space within a reporting interval; this is
+appropriate for the audio sequence stream and the 200 ms reporting period.
+
+## 2. Video frame accounting
+
+Count missing frames relative to the last processed frame, rather than repeatedly
+counting from the last successfully decoded frame. Count failed frames,
+completely missing frames and frames intentionally skipped during IDR recovery
+once, including across 65535 → 0. Count a rejected callback as a dropped frame.
+Saturate cumulative counters instead of allowing signed integer overflow.
+Initialize slice metadata before parsing it.
+
+## 3. Bounded IDR recovery
+
+Use a shared recovery state for automatic and manual IDR requests. Coalesce
+requests while recovery is active, including after an initial send failure.
+Retry at one-second intervals, with at most three submission attempts in a
+recovery episode. The existing one-second heartbeat polls recovery even without
+new video packets. Scheduling can delay a poll, so the timeout is acted upon at
+the first heartbeat at/after three seconds; it is not a hard real-time deadline.
+
+On timeout, stop suppressing frames and resume the existing reference-frame
+recovery path. This bounds the IDR gate; it does not guarantee that corrupted
+video can be decoded. No automatic reconnect, GPU reset or decoder recreation
+is introduced. An arriving recognized I-frame ends the wait and clears the
+receiver's stale reference history. Unparseable slices cannot bypass the gate.
+The I-frame recognition follows the existing parser; this batch does not expand
+its supported H.264/H.265 NAL types.
+
+## Validation
+
+- Core library compiled on Linux x86_64 with FFmpeg enabled (FFmpeg 6.1.1),
+  Qt GUI and CLI disabled.
+- General unit suite: 124/124 passed, including three new packet-statistics tests.
+- Isolated receiver suite: 5/5 passed with real frame processing, FEC and
+  bitstream parsing, plus a deterministic clock and fake transport.
+- Eight focused tests passed with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
+  LeakSanitizer was disabled because process inspection is restricted here.
+- No PS5, live network stream, GPU rendering, Gaming Mode, GUI compilation or
+  end-to-end latency measurement was available for this batch.
+
+The isolated receiver suite covers repeated FEC failures, missing frames,
+sequence wrap, callback rejection, counter saturation, automatic-recovery
+opt-in, send failure, request coalescing, retry spacing, timeout, malformed
+slices and recovery on H.264/H.265 I-frame fixtures.
+
+## Remaining priorities
+
+4. Separate network reception from decoder submission with explicit memory
+   ownership, bounded work and dependency-aware overflow recovery.
+5. Stabilize the video clock and coalesce Qt delivery/presentation notifications;
+   verify session lifetime and hardware-frame ownership across shutdown.
+6. Tune video reordering/audio buffering using measured jitter and frame age.
+
+These architectural changes are not implemented in this first batch. In
+particular, buffer sizes and cadence heuristics should not be tuned from unit
+tests alone. Establish the corrected baseline first and compare recordings of
+queue age, socket drops, decode time and presentation time during PS5 sessions.
